# Hypothesis: prevalence of gardening-introduced non-native plants by habitat

Prevalence of gardening-introduced (ornamental) non-native plants varies strongly with habitat type: it is highest in human-used lowland habitats (mean elevation of the four top habitats 176-386 m a.s.l.) and lowest in croplands and in the species-poor natural/semi-natural habitats. In the plot dataset (invaded_niche_pathways.csv; 632 plots, 481 species-occurrence records in n.total, 169 of them n.gard) gardening records concentrate in Wetlands (50.0% of 38 plots with n.gard>0, mean n.gard = 0.76, share = 29/52 = 0.56), Urban (47.7% of 65 plots with n.gard>0, mean n.gard = 0.69, share = 45/151 = 0.30), Coastal (44.8% of 29 plots with n.gard>0, mean n.gard = 0.79, share = 23/43 = 0.53), Riparian (36.1% of 61 plots with n.gard>0, mean n.gard = 0.57, share = 35/96 = 0.36). These four habitat types hold 78% of all n.gard records while covering only 31% of the plots. Croplands are gardening-poor and unintentional-dominated (n.gard = 20 of n.total = 108, share 0.19, occupancy 20.7%, while n.unint = 83), and gardening is rare in Meadows (n.gard = 4 of 13, occupancy 7.7%). The four habitats with fewer than 10 records (Coniferous forests 8, Broad-leaved forests 4, Scrublands 5, Rock outcrops 1; 308 plots in total) are gardening-rare per plot (occupancy 0-6.5%): their formal shares of 0.875 and 1.000 rest on 8 and 4 records and must not be read as 'forests are mainly garden plants'. Urban carries the largest absolute number of gardening records (45 of 169, 27%) but only a mid-range share (0.30), because unintentional introductions dominate there (104 of 151 records).

Secondary nuance: within the species-poor seminatural habitats (data-poor group: Coniferous and Broad-leaved forests, Scrublands, Rock outcrops) gardening accounts for 13 of only 18 records (72.2%, Fisher exact vs all other habitats, Holm p = 0.009), i.e. when an alien species does occur there it is usually a garden escape - but this rests on 18 records over 308 plots and coexists with a plot-level occupancy of only 3.3%. Croplands show the opposite (18.5% gardening, Holm p = 0.00023; 83 of 108 records are unintentional). At the species level (77 unique species, 39 gardening; 42 'Gardening' rows once the 4 pathway-conflicting duplicates are counted twice) gardening species are not broader in habitat.range (mean 2.00 vs 2.45, MWU p = 0.437) nor more widely distributed in number.of.invaded.plots (3.97 vs 7.82, p = 0.271), so the habitat contrast is a property of the habitats and of human land use rather than of inherently generalist garden species.

## Statistical support
Statistical support: permutation test of habitat x pathway over the 481 records (20000 permutations, seed 20261022) chi2 = 42.09, p_perm = 0.00005 (asymptotic chi2 p = 1.77e-07, min expected count 4.57); Kruskal-Wallis on n.gard per plot H = 139.56, p = 1.24e-27; quasi-binomial GLM n.gard/n.total ~ habitat with weights n.total, joint habitat F = 16.44, p = 3.09e-17. The habitat effect survives addition of urban.2009.1000m and elevation (Wald F = 17.50, p = 2.49e-18), so the association is not merely a proxy for local urbanisation or climate, but the hypothesis is descriptive, not causal. Sensitivity: restricting to the 191 plots with n.total > 0 (treating the 441 zero plots as true absences vs as non-observations) leaves the substantive gradient unchanged - Wetlands stays rank 1 and Croplands last among the six data-rich habitats, the share ranking is identical (Spearman rho = 1.000) and intensity ranking is stable (mean n.gard rho = 0.886, p = 0.019); only the occupancy ranks of Urban (2 -> 5) and Meadows (6 -> 4) shift, which reflects the changed denominator, not a reversal of the habitat pattern.

